    {
      "sha": "0df86e21c9a7e2f0de4177ab78b51fad47c805e4",
      "filename": "docs/seps/2549-TTL-for-list-results.mdx",
      "status": "modified",
      "additions": 9,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changes": 10,
      "blob_url": "https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/75db1e987cbbba6d170315dc99d0dfc440754aef/docs%2Fseps%2F2549-TTL-for-list-results.mdx",
      "raw_url": "https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/raw/75db1e987cbbba6d170315dc99d0dfc440754aef/docs%2Fseps%2F2549-TTL-for-list-results.mdx",
      "contents_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/contents/docs%2Fseps%2F2549-TTL-for-list-results.mdx?ref=75db1e987cbbba6d170315dc99d0dfc440754aef",
      "patch": "@@ -13,6 +13,14 @@ description: \"TTL for List Results\"\n   </Badge>\n </div>\n \n+<Note>\n+  This SEP has reached Final status and is preserved as a historical record of\n+  the design as accepted. Changes made to the protocol after finalization are\n+  not reflected here. Refer to the [current\n+  specification](/specification/latest) and its changelog for authoritative\n+  requirements.\n+</Note>\n+\n | Field         | Value                                                                           |\n | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n | **SEP**       | 2549                                                                            |\n@@ -220,4 +228,4 @@ _No reference implementation yet._\n \n ## Security Implications\n \n-A misconfigured or malicious serer could set an excessively long TTL, causing clients to cache stale data for longer than desired. However, since the TTL is a hint and clients can choose to ignore it or re-fetch if they suspect changes, the security risk is minimal. Clients should be designed to handle unexpected TTL values gracefully.\n+A misconfigured or malicious server could set an excessively long TTL, causing clients to cache stale data for longer than desired. However, since the TTL is a hint and clients can choose to ignore it or re-fetch if they suspect changes, the security risk is minimal. Clients should be designed to handle unexpected TTL values gracefully."
    },
